# AMI LwM2M Node — Application Kconfig

menu "AMI Configuration"

config AMI_SINGLE_PHASE
	bool "Single-phase meter mode"
	default y
	help
	  Enable for single-phase meters (e.g. Microstar residential).
	  Pre-skips Phase S and Phase T OBIS codes at startup to
	  avoid wasting ~5 seconds polling unsupported registers.
	  When disabled (3-phase mode), all 28 OBIS codes are polled.

config AMI_DEMO_MODE
	bool "Demo mode (simulated meter data)"
	default n
	help
	  Run without a physical DLMS/RS485 meter.
	  The node generates realistic synthetic readings and publishes
	  them through LwM2M Object 10242 so end-to-end Thread +
	  ThingsBoard validation can be performed in lab demos.

config AMI_LWM2M_RECOVER_BACKOFF_MIN_S
	int "Initial backoff between LwM2M recovery attempts (seconds)"
	default 60
	help
	  Base of the exponential backoff applied by lwm2m_recover_work_fn
	  when the LwM2M RD client cannot re-register. Doubles each failed
	  attempt up to RECOVER_BACKOFF_MAX_S.

	  60s is conservative enough to give the server time to recover from
	  a GC pause, restart, or temporary network blip without hammering it.

config AMI_LWM2M_RECOVER_BACKOFF_MAX_S
	int "Maximum backoff between LwM2M recovery attempts (seconds)"
	default 300
	help
	  Cap on the exponential backoff. After 60s → 120s → 240s → 300s
	  (capped). Beyond MAX_ATTEMPTS, the node escalates to sys_reboot.

config AMI_LWM2M_RECOVER_MAX_ATTEMPTS
	int "Max LwM2M recovery attempts before sys_reboot (warm)"
	default 5
	help
	  After this many consecutive failed attempts to re-register the
	  LwM2M client, the node performs a SYS_REBOOT_WARM. The Thread
	  session is preserved (warm reboot keeps NVS) so re-attach is fast,
	  but the LwM2M engine state is fully reset. Typical safety net for
	  pathological zombie states (Thread OK but LwM2M wedged).

	  Counter resets ONLY on LWM2M_RD_CLIENT_EVENT_REGISTRATION_COMPLETE
	  — UPDATE_COMPLETE does NOT reset it. This is the most conservative
	  policy: if the client repeatedly registers and falls back into
	  failure, escalation still happens.

config AMI_BOOT_REGISTER_DEADLINE_S
	int "Boot watchdog deadline: max seconds between boot and first REGISTER COMPLETE"
	default 90
	help
	  PRIO 8 boot watchdog. From the moment the firmware enters main loop
	  to the first LWM2M_RD_CLIENT_EVENT_REGISTRATION_COMPLETE event, this
	  is the maximum allowed time. If exceeded, the node performs a
	  SYS_REBOOT_WARM (preserving Thread NVS).

	  This is defense-in-depth for Bug E: nodes that hang in the boot
	  path before reaching lwm2m_rd_client_start (Thread attach stuck,
	  DNS-SD lookup repeatedly failing, init crash, etc) will auto-recover
	  without operator intervention.

	  Default 90s budget: 30s Thread attach + 5s IPv6 propagation +
	  10x7s DNS-SD retries + 30s REGISTER + safety margin. If actual
	  measured boot time exceeds this, raise the value before lowering
	  the watchdog gracefully.

	  Setting to 0 disables the boot watchdog (not recommended in fleet).

config AMI_TX_POWER_DBM
	int "OpenThread TX power (dBm)"
	default 8
	range -16 20
	help
	  Power output for the IEEE 802.15.4 radio. Lower values reduce the
	  ESP32-C6 self-heating significantly: each +3 dBm roughly doubles
	  RF output and adds ~20-30 mA of current draw during TX bursts,
	  which translates directly to die temperature. With nodes within
	  ~5 meters of the OTBR (typical bench / cabinet deployment), 8 dBm
	  is more than enough — RSSI of -32 to -45 dBm at the OTBR confirms.

	  Lower this further (3 or 0) if the cabinet is small and nodes
	  cluster <50 cm apart. Raise to 20 only for outdoor or long-range
	  deployments.

config AMI_RGB_AUTO_OFF_AFTER_REGISTER_S
	int "Seconds to keep RGB LED green after first REGISTER, then auto-off"
	default 60
	help
	  Defense-in-depth against self-heating from the WS2812 RGB LED.
	  After the first LWM2M_RD_CLIENT_EVENT_REGISTRATION_COMPLETE the
	  LED stays green for this many seconds, then turns OFF to save
	  power and reduce die heating. Errors (RED) and boot states
	  (BLUE/CYAN) still drive the LED — only steady-state GREEN is
	  auto-turned-off.

	  Set 0 to keep the LED ON forever (legacy behavior).

config AMI_REBOOT_USB_DRAIN_MS
	int "Milliseconds to sleep before sys_reboot to drain USB Serial/JTAG"
	default 5000
	help
	  ESP32-C6 native USB Serial/JTAG often hangs (host-side enumerated
	  but cannot open) when sys_reboot() races with an active console
	  session. Sleeping before the reboot gives the host time to detect
	  link drop and clean up its USB stack so the post-reset enumeration
	  succeeds without manual unplug.

	  5000 ms is conservative; 1500-2000 ms also works in practice but
	  loses log buffer flushing margin. Set to 0 to disable the delay.

config AMI_LWM2M_NOTIFY_MIN_INTERVAL_MS
	int "Per-resource minimum interval between LwM2M Notifies (milliseconds)"
	default 30000
	help
	  Firmware-side defense-in-depth throttle: each LwM2M resource of the
	  3-Phase Power Meter Object (10242) emits at most one Notify every
	  this-many milliseconds, regardless of how fast the DLMS poll updates
	  values or whether the server has configured pmin via Write-Attributes.

	  Standard pattern in industrial deployments (Apple HomeKit, Nordic
	  nRF Cloud) — independent of any server-side rate control.

	  Sane values:
	    30000  default. ~1 notify per resource per 30s. With 14 resources
	           and 30 nodes ≈ 14/min/node × 30 = 420/min total to TB Edge.
	    60000  more conservative for very large fleets (>60 nodes).
	    < 5000 aggressive — can saturate the Thread mesh with >20 nodes.
	    0      disable throttle (NOT recommended for production).

	  When the server eventually sends Write-Attributes with pmin, the
	  effective rate is MAX(server_pmin, this_local_minimum). Our path
	  always checks this minimum first; Zephyr's LwM2M engine independently
	  honors any pmin attribute set on the resource. So this Kconfig acts
	  as a hard floor.

	  Per-mesh override: set in overlays/<mesh>.conf if needed.

config AMI_LWM2M_SERVER_IPV6_PRIMARY
	string "DEPRECATED — Primary LwM2M server IPv6 (no longer read by code)"
	default ""
	help
	  DEPRECATED in v0.6.0. Server discovery is DNS-SD only via
	  _lwm2m._udp.default.service.arpa. The firmware ignores this value
	  even if set. The Kconfig symbol is preserved so legacy overlays
	  do not fail to build, but its value has no effect.

	  Remove from your overlay file. v0.7.0 will delete the symbol.

config AMI_LWM2M_SERVER_IPV6_SECONDARY
	string "DEPRECATED — Secondary LwM2M server IPv6 (no longer read by code)"
	default ""
	help
	  DEPRECATED in v0.6.0. See AMI_LWM2M_SERVER_IPV6_PRIMARY.

config AMI_LWM2M_DNS_SD_RETRY_MAX
	int "Maximum DNS-SD lookup retries before sys_reboot at boot"
	default 10
	help
	  Number of consecutive DNS-SD failures at boot before the node
	  performs sys_reboot(SYS_REBOOT_WARM). Each retry uses exponential
	  backoff (5s, 10s, 20s, ..., capped at 60s). Default 10 → max ~9 min
	  before reboot. Tunable for harsh mesh-attach environments.

config AMI_LWM2M_DNS_SD_TIMEOUT_MS
	int "Per-attempt DNS-SD lookup timeout (milliseconds)"
	default 7000
	help
	  Timeout for a single otDnsClientResolveService attempt. Below 5000
	  may starve under realistic mesh latency; above 15000 causes
	  sluggish boot if SRP server is unresponsive.

choice AMI_MESH
	prompt "Target Thread mesh"
	default AMI_MESH_R1000
	help
	  Selects which OTBR/Thread network this firmware will join.
	  The dataset TLV is hardcoded in src/main.c (apply_otbr_dataset)
	  to bypass NVS persistence — switching here selects which TLV is
	  compiled in.

	  Use overlays to combine with the device-role variant:
	    west build ... -- -DEXTRA_CONF_FILE="overlays/med.conf;overlays/r1000.conf"
	  or via tools/build_firmware.py:
	    python tools/build_firmware.py --variant med --mesh r1000

config AMI_MESH_R1000
	bool "UNAL-R1000 on Seeed R1000 (192.168.8.176, channel 21) — production default"
	help
	  Production deployment — clean channel, lessons learned applied.
	    Channel:        21
	    PAN ID:         0x41ae
	    Network Name:   UNAL-R1000
	    Mesh-local:     fdf1:a391:6243:2a67::/64
	    LwM2M server:   fdf1:a391:6243:2a67:2478:c089:bf5a:2554 (mleid R1000)
	    LwM2M lifetime: 120s (set in overlays/r1000.conf)
	  See docs/architecture/edge-r1000.md for full spec.

config AMI_MESH_PI4
	bool "UNAL-Thread on Pi4 EKH01 (192.168.1.111, channel 25) — legacy"
	help
	  Legacy deployment — kept for migration of existing nodes.
	    Channel:        25
	    PAN ID:         0x23ed
	    Network Name:   UNAL-Thread
	    Mesh-local:     fdf5:bffd:0bd6:ef74::/64
	    LwM2M server:   fdf5:bffd:bd6:ef74:eb7b:f1d1:874e:2b91 (mleid Pi4)
	    LwM2M lifetime: 300s (prj.conf default, no per-mesh override)
	  Note: this network has documented contention with rogue PAN 0xe702
	  on channel 25 — see docs/architecture/edge-thingsboard.md.

endchoice

endmenu

source "Kconfig.zephyr"
